1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,230
Your retry fix can stop duplicates…

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:05,400
and still lose a customer’s order.

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:06,460
Let me show you.

4
00:00:07,324 --> 00:00:10,734
This began with a real AWS experiment.

5
00:00:11,454 --> 00:00:15,114
An SQS worker called
fulfillment successfully,

6
00:00:15,674 --> 00:00:17,194
then the Lambda crashed.

7
00:00:17,294 --> 00:00:21,294
When the message returned, the
unsafe handler fulfilled it again.

8
00:00:21,854 --> 00:00:25,474
A separate DynamoDB receipt
check caught the duplicate.

9
00:00:25,987 --> 00:00:27,934
The repair left one receipt.

10
00:00:28,335 --> 00:00:30,354
That run is preserved here.

11
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,040
Now watch the harder case.

12
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:34,860
Two valid orders share a product.

13
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:39,080
With no key, both orders
get duplicate receipts.

14
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:43,000
Use the product as the key, and
the second order gets nothing.

15
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:45,370
One receipt, zero receipts.

16
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:46,860
That is not a repair.

17
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:51,494
Use the business order instead:
one receipt for each customer.

18
00:00:52,234 --> 00:00:57,494
The test checks both promises—no
duplicates, and no missing valid order.

19
00:00:57,917 --> 00:01:01,174
Let’s try it with code. This
portable test runs locally.

20
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:05,575
Its crash happens after
the receipt commits,

21
00:01:05,754 --> 00:01:07,094
before the response returns.

22
00:01:07,794 --> 00:01:10,991
Download the kit and open
the small Python adapter.

23
00:01:11,571 --> 00:01:14,151
This line chooses the order ID.

24
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:17,631
The observer checks the
receiver’s receipts independently.

25
00:01:18,271 --> 00:01:20,969
I’ll run the crash-and-retry case first.

26
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:23,049
One receipt. Pass.

27
00:01:23,221 --> 00:01:29,274
Now remove the key.

28
00:01:29,304 --> 00:01:31,364
Same inputs, same fault. Run again.

29
00:01:31,916 --> 00:01:35,645
There it is: violation. Two receipts.

30
00:01:36,025 --> 00:01:39,625
Import the report, and the exact
order that broke is right here.

31
00:01:40,225 --> 00:01:42,865
Expand its evidence: the effect committed,

32
00:01:42,945 --> 00:01:46,905
the response was lost, and the
retry created another receipt.

33
00:01:47,476 --> 00:01:54,217
Put the business key back.

34
00:01:54,437 --> 00:01:56,967
Run the same case, import the new report…

35
00:01:56,997 --> 00:01:59,357
one receipt, and the check passes again.

36
00:02:00,182 --> 00:02:03,390
A separate synthetic
application, DispatchDesk,

37
00:02:03,610 --> 00:02:06,170
also uses this adapter contract.

38
00:02:06,770 --> 00:02:11,130
Its five frozen cases pass
with the reference repair.

39
00:02:12,004 --> 00:02:14,550
Remove the key, all five fail.

40
00:02:14,790 --> 00:02:18,170
Use only the product, and
two valid-order cases fail.

41
00:02:18,551 --> 00:02:21,739
Download the report and keep
the regression with your code.

42
00:02:22,039 --> 00:02:26,089
It carries the inputs,
fault plan, assertions,

43
00:02:26,119 --> 00:02:28,799
source hashes, and observed receipts.

44
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,834
Missing evidence stays incomplete.

45
00:02:32,694 --> 00:02:35,314
Unsupported effects stay unresolved.

46
00:02:35,869 --> 00:02:37,654
ReplayGuard.

47
00:02:38,014 --> 00:02:40,754
Keep the failure. Test the repair.
